/**

@page toolbox_particles Particles

\li \subpage toolbox_particles_memorypool
\li \subpage toolbox::particles::ParticleSet

Peano's particle toolbox provides features to add tracers to your code, but
also to add particles which swim around. We have some features to dump
Lagrangian data into data bases and some generic input file readers. However,
the toolbox has no domain-specific content, i.e. Physics. It is merely the
base of tracers in ExaHyPE and SPH particles in Swift 2 for example.

Particles in Peano are typically tied to the mesh through the
Particles-in-Dual-Tree paradigm which we introduce in our paper

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
       @article{Weinzierl:2016:PIDT,
         title = {Two particle-in-grid realisations on spacetrees},
         journal = {Parallel Computing},
         volume = {52},
         pages = {42-64},
         year = {2016},
         issn = {0167-8191},
         doi = {https://doi.org/10.1016/j.parco.2015.12.007},
         url = {https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0167819115001635},
         author = {T. Weinzierl and B. Verleye and P. Henri and D. Roose},
         keywords = {Particle-in-cell, Spacetree, Particle sorting, AMR, Lagrangian-Eulerian methods, Communication-avoiding},
         abstract = {The present paper studies two particle management strategies for dynamically adaptive Cartesian grids at hands of a particle-in-cell code. One holds the particles within the grid cells, the other within the grid vertices. The fundamental challenge for the algorithmic strategies results from the fact that particles may run through the grid without velocity constraints. To facilitate this, we rely on multiscale grid representations. They allow us to lift and drop particles between different spatial resolutions. We call this cell-based strategy particle in tree (PIT). Our second approach assigns particles to vertices describing a dual grid (PIDT) and augments the lifts and drops with multiscale linked cells. Our experiments validate the two schemes at hands of an electrostatic particle-in-cell code by retrieving the dispersion relation of Langmuir waves in a thermal plasma. They reveal that different particle and grid characteristics favour different realisations. The possibility that particles can tunnel through an arbitrary number of grid cells implies that most data is exchanged between neighbouring ranks, while very few data is transferred non-locally. This constraints the scalability as the code potentially has to realise global communication. We show that the merger of an analysed tree grammar with PIDT allows us to predict particle movements among several levels and to skip parts of this global communication a priori. It is capable to outperform several established implementations based upon trees and/or space-filling curves.}
       }
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


## Particle data model

As the toolbox is quite generic, it really only provides a very basic particle
model where the particle hosts only the very essential parameters. The particle
itself is modelled as a DaStGen class in Python within peano4.toolbox.particles.Particle.

Extensions and user codes typically take this baselien data model and augment
it with further attributes which carry the actual physics.


## Particle storage within mesh


The particles are stored following the dual tree storage paradigm.

@image html peano4/toolbox/particles/dual-tree.png

We hold the whole spacetree with all of its levels. This is similar to holding
a cascade of finer and finer, ragged Cartesian meshes. First, each particle is
assigned into the grid level within this tree that can just accommodate its
search diameter within one cell lenght. Particles therefore can reside on
different mesh levels. After that, we store each particle in the vertex closest
to its centre.


When we touch a vertex for the first time, we know all the particles close to
it. We can also look at particles further up the hierarchy. When we run into a
cell, we can sweep through its @f$ 2^d @f$ vertices and collect the particles
from there. This will give us a set of all particles within this cell plus the
ones which are up to $h/2$ away. We get kind of the cell data plus some halo
of half a cell width.


It is very common that codes want to run throught the mesh and do something
with all particles within one specific cell, or evaluate all particle-particle
interactions with particles in the neighbouring cells (or half of it) plus
all particles on coarser levels. Therefore, the toolbox provides the action
sets peano4.toolbox.particles.ParticleParticleInteraction_Sets and
peano4.toolbox.particles.ParticleParticleInteraction_StackOfLists. These are
two different implementations of the same "tree walker" algorithms which
eventually provides pseudo-Verlet lists distilled from the tree structure.


To realise this vertex-based storage, each vertex is associated with a particle
set. A particle set has to have a certain signature, which is similar to a C++
standard container. Notably, it exposes all the particles as pointers. So,
logically, a vertex holds a sequence of pointers to particles. This does not
mean that the particles are scattered all over the heap. Having particles
scattered is the default storage, but you can optimise the storage. Logically,
this means that you work @ref toolbox_particles_memorypool "with a particle pool".
You still pretend that each and every particle is represented by a pointer to
the heap and that you can, consequently, build lists of pointers which might
hold totally scattered memory addresses. Internally, a pool however might
ensure that these totally scattered addresses are properly stored in memory and
fragmentation and a lot of cache misses are avoided.


## Particle list flavours

The particle set flavours can be controlled via the generator object within
peano4.toolbox.particles.ParticleSet. Consult the documentation there. 
Each particle set, from the outside, is a collection of pointers to the 
real particles. The
actual realisation of the sets then is guided by a few simple principles:

### Standard vertex operations

These are operations each vertex offers:

  - send(const peano4::datamanagement::VertexMarker& marker)
  - receiveAndMerge(const peano4::datamanagement::VertexMarker& marker)
  - storePersistently(const peano4::datamanagement::VertexMarker& marker)
  - loadPersistently(const peano4::datamanagement::VertexMarker& marker)
  - If we run in debug mode, we further require the x and h values
  - A toString() routine


### clear(), deleteParticle() and deleteParticles()

The operation clear() on a particle set becomes a shallow clear, as the set
administers pointers to particles. The indices are destroyed, the particles
themselves remain there. If another stack references the same particles, you
neither have a leak nor will you induce an invalid memory access.

The routine deleteParticle() allows you to actually delete a particle (and 
to remove it from the list). This routine has to be handled with care. If 
someone else holds a pointer to the underlying particle, this pointer all at
a sudden becomes invalid without any further notification.

deleteParticles() is a shortcut to delete all particles associated with a 
vertex/stack entry.


### move() and add()


### Multiscale transitions

The routine particleCanNotBeLiftedLocally() informs the vertex that the 
particle associated with it should not be linked to it anymore. However, the
code also fails to associate it directly with another vertex, as this other
vertex that would be required is not available within the local subdomain.


This routine basically takes the particle and inserts it into the list
of particles that the current tree cannot re-sort locally. This list is 
represented by an instance of toolbox::particles::SieveParticles, which is
a class attribute of the toolbox::particles::Particle superclass. 

The tree traversal handles all particles independent of their status.
However, it makes no sense to lift virtual particles, as they are
copies of another rank and therefore this other rank has the
responsibility to handle them. Whenever this routine is called for
a virtual particle, I hence should ignore the lift/sieve request. We can safely
assume that any correct code calls this routine for local particles only.

The fact that we ignore virtual particles mirrors the fact that we
never send out virtual particles that run through the domain boundary.
Actually, we do send these guys out, but they will be ignored on the
receiver side.

The routine is similar to a shallow copy. The set is only stripped of its
link to the particle. In return, the global sieve link is added this pointer.


Each rank will assemble its own set of particles by calling this routine. 
These rank-local lists have to be consolidated (all reduce) after each step. 
The reduction triggered by finishedTraversal() which invokes
toolbox::particles::SieveParticles::exchangeSieveListsGlobally().
It is hence important that finishedTraversal() on the sieve list is called
after each grid sweep which might lift particles. Otherwise, the subsequent 
sweep cannot sieve the particles correctly.

The operation is not in itself thread-safe, but you may assume that the 
underlying toolbox::particles::SieveParticles::addParticleThatCanNotBeLiftedWithinItsTree() 
is thread-safe.

The routine returns an iterator. So if you iterate over a set and if you call
this operation, you should take the result as new iterator, as it will point
to a consistent memory view.



### Eliminate spatial duplicates

The routine eliminateSpatialDuplicates() has to be offered by each vertex 
set. Run over set and removes one of the two particles that reside at same 
location.

Such an eliminate of duplicates can be motivated through the merger, where it 
can happen that multiple ranks tell us about a particle (some of them have 
virtual copies, some have local ones), as the association of particles to trees
is not unique. After all, we work with floating point arithmetics. Those are 
the bad guys. Keeping redundant particles will introduce wrong physics (doubled
forces for example) in many cases, or even lead to crashes (division by zero).

This elimination means that I actually delete the particle held by
particleSet. Data is actually freed.

Please consult the discussion on the merger below for some more explanations
where and why we need this elimination routine.


### Clone

The operation clone() is a deep clone. We get data from somewhere else and 
create our own version with which we can work from hereon. Besides the 
particles, we also copy (aka clone) the debug x and h values.
The clone itself is mechanical. The actual logic how to keep different cloned 
stacks consistent is all buried within merge().


### Merge


The operation merge() merges an incoming particle set from neighbour into the 
local set. We run through neighbour and assess what to do with the particles
therein. Basically, we look at its state and then decide whether to
enqueue the particle here, too. Furthermore, we do some state switches.
If a particle is local on another rank for example, it has to be
virtual here.

It is important to study this routine in combination with the (Python)
action set peano4.toolbox.particles.UpdateParallelState which is the
routine that actually manages the parallel lifecycle.

The merge works with shallow copies. We assume that all necessary real
copying has been done before. See peano4::parallel::SpacetreeSet::exchangeAllHorizontalDataExchangeStacks()
for a discussion of deep copying in this context.

If we decide not to insert an incoming particle into our local data
structure, we have to destroy it, as the containers for boundary data
exchange perform deep copies.

We receive particle sets from other trees. These are sent out after
particles have been touched for the last time there. Therefore,
their parallel state has already changed and reflects the latest
state. It makes no sense to study the new and the current state as
they now will be the same. Logically, it is the current state only
which determines what type of particle we have here, as this is the
one that has overwritten the other state.

In an ideal world, the sending rank would eliminate all of its virtual
local copies of particles before it sends its stuff out. So it would
send out only its local particles. In practice, we do not rely on this
fact and just ignore incoming particles that have been flagged as
virtual by their sender. This means that our neighbour had a halo
particle (which it must have gotten from somewhere) and now sends this
one back to us. We just ignore it (mind the deletion).

It makes no sense to set the new state of the particle here, as the
merge always precedes the actual first touch of a particle where the
new state is reset anyway.

If a particle moves from one domain into the other, Peano will
distribute the particle copy among all the other guys and all other
trees will first assume that the particle is local. If it is not, they
will set it to virtual and consequently remove it. In-between they
exchange their local (assumed) particles, so we end up with a particle
accumulation on one rank: Multiple copies of the virtual particle drop
in. One per tree which assumes it were its own local copy. So I have
to remove duplicates, i.e. ensure that we don't insert duplicates here.

The other redundancy results from two adjacent cells on different
trees and a vertex in-between which hosts a particle which is actually
completely outside of the domain. We need two grid sweeps to eliminate
such guys. See the action set UpdateParallelState. In-between, both
adjacent trees send their virtual copy to the counterpart, so we
accumulate local particles and get more and more. We have to eliminate
replicas asap.

I started with an implementation which first inserts all the particles
into the vertex and then eliminates those guys which are redundant.
This turns out to be a slow version. It is way faster to find those
guys which are to be added and then to check prior to the insert if
they really should be added or not.


### Constructors

The copy constructor is a shallow copy constructor.


 */
 
